WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.152
We raced through the woods toward the stockade, and we could hear the pirates running and shouting as they drew nearer.

2
00:00:07.153 --> 00:00:10.572
Branches cracked when the pirates crossed the thicket;

3
00:00:10.573 --> 00:00:15.550
I realized we were going to have a close brush with them, so I prepared my pistol.

4
00:00:15.551 --> 00:00:18.931
"Captain," I said, "Trelawney is our best marksman.

5
00:00:18.932 --> 00:00:22.565
Give him your gun. His gun is useless."

6
00:00:22.566 --> 00:00:29.355
They exchanged guns, and Trelawney, silent and cool, checked to see that the weapon was ready to be fired.

7
00:00:29.356 --> 00:00:34.025
"Here’s my cutlass, Gray," I said when I observed he was unarmed.

8
00:00:34.026 --> 00:00:39.579
He spat in his hand, knit his brows, and made the blade sing through the air.

9
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:44.062
I could see our new man would be a valuable addition.

10
00:00:44.063 --> 00:00:49.582
Forty paces farther, we saw the stockade and approached it on the southeastern side.

11
00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:57.199
Almost at the same time, seven mutineers—with Job leading them—appeared at the southwestern corner.

12
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:04.261
In the stockade, Hunter and Joyce started firing their weapons, and the squire and I stood where we were and fired

13
00:01:04.262 --> 00:01:09.797
too. One of the pirates fell, and the rest immediately plunged into the

14
00:01:09.798 --> 00:01:14.548
trees. After reloading, we walked around the fence to find the fallen

15
00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:17.849
enemy. He was stone dead—shot through the

16
00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:26.217
heart. We rejoiced over our good success, but at that moment, a pistol shot rang from the bushes and a bullet whistled past my

17
00:01:26.218 --> 00:01:29.946
ear. Poor Tom Redruth stumbled and

18
00:01:29.947 --> 00:01:39.149
fell. Both the squire and I returned the shot, but we were only wasting powder, so after we reloaded, we turned our attention to

19
00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:45.469
Tom. The captain and Gray were examining him, and I immediately saw that he wasn’t going to

20
00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:53.872
live. The pirates had stopped firing, so we hoisted the gamekeeper over the stockade and carried him, groaning and bleeding, into the log

21
00:01:53.873 --> 00:02:02.773
house. The poor old fellow, who had never uttered one word of surprise, complaint, or fear, was laid down in the log

22
00:02:02.774 --> 00:02:07.226
house. He was the oldest among us, and now he was going to

23
00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:12.349
die. The squire dropped down beside him on his knees and kissed his hand,

24
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:16.494
crying. "Am I going, Doctor?" Redruth

25
00:02:16.495 --> 00:02:21.174
asked. "Tom, my man," I said, "you’re going

26
00:02:21.175 --> 00:02:26.987
home." "I wish I could have shot one of them first," Redruth

27
00:02:26.988 --> 00:02:32.380
murmured. While Tom was dying, the captain pulled a great many items out of his own

28
00:02:32.381 --> 00:02:39.595
pockets—two British flags, a Bible, a coil of rope, pen, ink, the logbook, and tobacco.

29
00:02:39.596 --> 00:02:47.961
Outside the house, he found a tall fir tree that had been chopped down, and with the help of Hunter, he set it up at the corner of the house.

30
00:02:47.962 --> 00:02:51.598
Then he climbed onto the roof and ran up the flag.

31
00:02:51.599 --> 00:02:58.738
Cheered by the sight of the Union Jack, the captain re-entered the log house and started counting up all the provisions.

32
00:02:58.739 --> 00:03:04.105
"Tom," the squire said, "tell me you forgive me, won’t you?"

33
00:03:04.106 --> 00:03:17.126
"There’s nothing to forgive. I have great respect for you, sir . . . that’s all, amen!" he replied and, without another word, he passed

34
00:03:17.127 --> 00:03:24.151
away. When Tom died, Captain Smollett came forward with another flag and reverently spread it on the

35
00:03:24.152 --> 00:03:28.997
body. "He was a good man," the captain said to the squire as he shook his

36
00:03:28.998 --> 00:03:35.033
hand. "He died doing his duty. Let’s hope we will all meet death in the same brave

37
00:03:35.034 --> 00:03:44.115
way." Then the captain pulled me aside. "Dr. Livesey," he said, "When do you and the squire expect anyone to come for us? A few

38
00:03:44.116 --> 00:03:52.199
weeks?" "No, months," I replied. "I told Blandly that he was to send a ship if we were not back by the end of August, but not

39
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:58.393
sooner." "Well," said the captain, "even if we’re very lucky, I should say we are in serious

40
00:03:58.394 --> 00:04:01.369
trouble." "What do you mean?" I

41
00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:05.518
asked. "It’s a pity we lost that second boatload," the captain

42
00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:08.740
said. "As for powder and shot, we have

43
00:04:08.741 --> 00:04:17.298
enough. But the food rations are short, very short—so short, Dr. Livesey, that we’re better off without that extra mouth to

44
00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:21.074
feed." And he pointed to the dead body under the

45
00:04:21.075 --> 00:04:31.211
flag. Just then, with a roar and a whistle, a cannonball passed over the roof of the log house and fell inside the stockade, scattering sand but doing no

46
00:04:31.212 --> 00:04:36.255
damage. "Captain," said Trelawney, "the house is quite invisible from the

47
00:04:36.256 --> 00:04:41.979
ship. It must be the flag they are aiming at. Wouldn’t it be wiser to take it

48
00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:47.487
down?" "Take down the British flag?" the captain cried. "No, sir, I will

49
00:04:47.488 --> 00:04:56.280
not." As soon as he said it, I think we all agreed with him, because the flag improved our morale and showed our enemies that we despised

50
00:04:56.281 --> 00:04:59.112
them. All through the evening, they kept thundering

51
00:04:59.113 --> 00:05:05.414
away. Ball after ball flew over, fell short, or kicked up the sand in the

52
00:05:05.415 --> 00:05:12.626
enclosure. The pirates had to fire high, so most shots completely missed their mark or buried themselves in the

53
00:05:12.627 --> 00:05:14.980
soft sand. The balls didn’t bounce in

54
00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:20.592
different directions, and though one popped in through the roof of the log house and out again through the floor, we soon got used

55
00:05:20.593 --> 00:05:25.950
to it. The captain sat down to his log, and here is the beginning of

56
00:05:25.951 --> 00:05:35.204
the entry: Alexander Smollett, captain; David Livesey, ship’s doctor; Abraham Gray, carpenter’s mate; John

57
00:05:35.205 --> 00:05:42.502
Trelawney, owner; John Hunter and Richard Joyce, owner’s servants—being all that is left of the faithful ship’s

58
00:05:42.503 --> 00:05:50.291
company—with stores for ten days at short rations, came ashore this day and flew British colors on the log house on Treasure Island.

59
00:05:50.292 --> 00:05:57.342
Thomas Redruth, owner’s servant, shot by the mutineers; James Hawkins, cabin boy—

60
00:05:57.343 --> 00:06:04.122
As he was writing, I was wondering about poor Jim Hawkins. Just then we heard someone shouting.

61
00:06:04.123 --> 00:06:10.211
"Doctor! Squire! Captain! Hello, Mr. Hunter, is that you?"

62
00:06:10.212 --> 00:06:19.654
And I ran to the door to see Jim Hawkins, safe and sound, climbing over the stockade.

